WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:16.032
A pedigree is a family tree. A square is a male, and a circle is a female. A shaded shape has the condition. A line joins two parents, and a line down leads to their children. Always read the key.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:36.988
An allele is one form of a gene. A genotype is the pair of alleles a person has. This condition is caused by a recessive allele, small a. A recessive allele is hidden when the dominant allele, capital A, is with it. So every shaded person is small a, small a. Person 3 is small a, small a.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:57.283
Now the parents. Person 3 got one small a from each parent. But both parents are unshaded, so each also has a capital A. So persons 1 and 2 are both capital A, small a: carriers. A carrier has the recessive allele but not the condition.

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:09.085
Person 4 is unshaded, so he has at least one capital A. He could be capital A, capital A, or capital A, small a. When two genotypes fit, give both.

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:28.992
If the condition is dominant, capital H, flip the rule. A dominant allele shows even with only one copy. Unshaded people have no capital H, so they are small h, small h. A shaded person with an unshaded parent or child is capital H, small h.

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:44.624
For a sex-linked condition, write the X and the Y. An affected son is X small h, Y. He got his X from his mother. She is unshaded, so she is a carrier: X capital H, X small h. For a Y-linked trait, only males have it, on the Y.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:02:04.916
Blood groups have three alleles: I-A, I-B and I-O. I-A and I-B are codominant: both show. I-O is recessive. For group A, the genotype is I-A, I-A, or I-A, I-O. Group B is the same pattern. Group AB is I-A, I-B. Group O is only I-O, I-O. So a group O child means both parents carry I-O.

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:20.092
Here is a real exam question. Some people in this family have sickle cell anaemia. It is caused by a recessive allele. Give the genotype of person 6. One mark.

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:31.750
Here is the family tree. Squares are males and circles are females. Shaded means sickle cell anaemia. Person 6 is the unshaded square on the left.

00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:44.186
Person 6 is an unshaded square: a male without the condition. Look at his mother, person 2. She is shaded, so she is small a, small a. She can only pass on small a.

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:58.400
Person 6 is unshaded, so he has at least one capital A. He got small a from his mother. So person 6 is capital A, small a. Answer by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:12.314
A common mistake is to write capital A, capital A for an unshaded person with a shaded parent. The shaded parent gave them a small a, so they must be capital A, small a.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:21.152
Another mistake is to give only one genotype when two fit. For group A, give both: I-A, I-A, and I-A, I-O, unless the family rules one out.

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:35.031
Your turn. A mother has blood group A, and a father has blood group B. Their child has blood group O. Give the genotypes of all three people, and explain. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:44.629
Start with the child. Group O has only one genotype: I-O, I-O. So the child got one I-O from each parent.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:54.732
The mother is group A, so she has I-A. She also gave I-O, so she is I-A, I-O. The father is group B, so he has I-B, and he gave I-O. He is I-B, I-O.

00:03:55.300 --> 00:04:02.745
So, every time: read the key, write the certain genotypes first, then work out the rest from one allele in each parent. Give all that fit.
